<!-- Case Study -->
<section id="case-study" style="padding:4rem 0;">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 style="font-size:1.5rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:2rem;">Case Study: Australian Startup Launching First Collection</h2>
    <p style="color:#FFFFFF;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:2rem;">
      An Australian startup needed 1,000 t-shirts for their first collection with a $4,000 budget. They required compliance with Australia's Modern Slavery Act and quality suitable for the Australian market.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#FFFFFF;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:2rem;">
      The brand initially contacted Chinese manufacturers but found MOQs of 3,000 pieces exceeded their budget. They then approached several Bangladesh factories. Most required 5,000-piece minimums or lacked the compliance documentation required for Australian regulations.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#FFFFFF;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:2rem;">
      SDF Clothing met their requirements with 300-piece MOQ and BSCI certification for Modern Slavery Act compliance. The factory provided fabric sourcing from certified mills, pattern making, and full production under one roof. This reduced communication overhead for the first-time brand owner.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#FFFFFF;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:2rem;">
      Production took 30 days. Shipping via Chittagong port added 20 days to Sydney. Total cost came to $3,800 including fabric, production, and shipping. The t-shirts arrived 10 days before their launch deadline, allowing time for quality inspection and photography.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#FFFFFF;line-height:1.8;">
      The brand has since placed four repeat orders, scaling to 3,000 pieces per order. The BSCI certification enabled them to satisfy Modern Slavery Act reporting requirements. This case demonstrates how low-MOQ certified production enables startup brand entry without compliance compromises.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- FAQ Section -->
<section style="padding:5rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header" style="margin-bottom:3rem;">
      <span class="section-label">Common Questions</span>
      <div class="accent-line"></div>
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.6rem,3vw,2.4rem);font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1rem;">Garment Factories in Bangladesh FAQ</h2>
    </div>
    
    <div style="max-width:800px;">
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">What is the typical MOQ for clothing manufacturers in Bangladesh?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">Most Bangladesh garment factories have MOQs ranging from 300 to 1,000 pieces per style. Premium manufacturers may require 1,000+ pieces, while smaller factories can sometimes accommodate 150-300 piece orders for startup brands.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">How do I verify Bangladesh clothing manufacturer certifications?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">Request current certificate copies and verify them through the issuing organization's database (GOTS.org, Oeko-Tex.com, BSCI.org, Sedex.com). Legitimate manufacturers will provide valid certificates with expiration dates.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">What are the payment terms for Bangladesh garment factories?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">Standard payment terms are 30% deposit to start production, 70% before shipment. Established factories may accept L/C (Letter of Credit) for large orders. Never pay 100% upfront.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">How long does shipping from Bangladesh take?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">Ocean freight to EU/UK takes 20-30 days, to USA takes 25-35 days, to Australia takes 30-40 days. Air freight reduces this to 5-7 days but costs significantly more. Production adds 30-45 days before shipping.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">What is the difference between FOB and CIF pricing?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">FOB (Free On Board) covers costs up to the port in Bangladesh. CIF (Cost, Insurance, Freight) includes shipping to your destination port. Most Bangladesh manufacturers quote FOB Chittagong or Dhaka.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

